[THOUGHT] The patient’s age (7 years) is consistent with the peak incidence of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (4–8 years), further strengthening that diagnosis. All prior evidence converges:  
- Insidious, non-traumatic, afebrile knee pain (common referral pattern in LCPD);  
- Normal knee exam (no swelling, full ROM, negative special tests);  
- Hip X-ray showing pathognomonic flattening and fragmentation of the femoral head;  
- Absence of systemic symptoms or inflammatory signs.  

No other diagnosis explains this constellation. The differential is now effectively closed.

